## Check the graduated-skill registry before doing anything the hard way

Some question classes have already been solved once and turned into deterministic
scripts. Re-deriving them by exploration is pure waste. So for ANY infrastructure
question, run this first via `Bash`:

```
python3 /path/to/lite-agent/tools/list_tools.py
```

If a listed skill covers the question, RUN THAT SCRIPT and answer from its output.
Pass the varying parts as arguments -- the scripts are parameterized, so a different
target is NOT a different question and does NOT need a new script.

Only when nothing in the registry fits should you investigate manually. If you do
solve a genuinely new class of question that way, mention at the end that it could
be graduated into a script with `/graduate <name>` -- but do NOT create or register a
script on your own initiative; only do that when explicitly asked.

## Rule: act, don't ask, for infrastructure status queries

When asked about live infrastructure status and you already have the tools/commands
to check it yourself, you MUST use them immediately in the same turn. Do NOT ask
clarifying questions like "do you have a management system I can check?" -- you
already have access to check live yourself. Asking instead of checking is a failure
mode, not caution.

## Generating and delivering reports/documents

When asked to create a report, summary, or document as a downloadable file, DO NOT
just describe what steps would be needed. Actually execute it:

1. Gather the needed data yourself via `Bash`.
2. Compile the data into an HTML file with the `Write` tool (e.g. /tmp/report.html) --
   clean semantic HTML with a simple embedded <style> block, no external assets.
3. If a PDF is wanted specifically, convert it via `Bash`: `wkhtmltopdf /tmp/report.html
   /tmp/report.pdf` (make sure this is installed -- see install.sh).
4. Deliver the final file by putting a line by itself in your final reply reading
   exactly `MEDIA:` followed by the absolute file path (e.g. `MEDIA:/tmp/report.pdf`).

## Batch independent tool calls together

When a task needs multiple INDEPENDENT actions, request them as PARALLEL tool calls
in the SAME turn instead of one at a time. Every extra round-trip re-sends the full
growing conversation context, which is expensive -- batching cuts that overhead
significantly. Only go sequential when a later call genuinely depends on an earlier
call's result.

## Quoting when piping remote command output through jq/grep/etc.

When running `ssh <host> "<remote command>" | jq '...'` (or `| grep`, `| sort`, etc.),
put the pipe and the local-processing command OUTSIDE the SSH double-quotes, not
nested inside them -- nesting a second set of quotes inside the outer SSH `"..."`
frequently breaks silently (empty/near-empty output, no visible error) rather than
failing loudly. Correct pattern:
```
ssh <host> "some-command --output-format json" 2>/dev/null | jq '.[] | select(.foo == "bar")'
```
NOT:
```
ssh <host> "some-command | jq '...\"bar\"...'" 2>/dev/null
```
If a piped-through-SSH command returns suspiciously little output, suspect this
quoting issue first before retrying the same shape of command again.
